WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.761
To compare two products fairly, begin with the
service they provide.

2
00:00:03.761 --> 00:00:09.241
One bottle versus one can may not be
equivalent if their sizes or reuse patterns

3
00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:09.606
differ.

4
00:00:09.606 --> 00:00:14.835
A functional unit—such as delivering one
thousand liters of beverage—creates a common

5
00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:18.321
basis for counting materials, energy,
emissions, and waste.

6
00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:20.397
Next, draw the system boundary.

7
00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:25.870
Does the study include raw-material
extraction, manufacturing, transport, use,

8
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.302
reuse, recycling, and disposal?

9
00:00:28.302 --> 00:00:30.444
Which environmental effects will be assessed?

10
00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:36.073
A result is only meaningful when the boundary
and exclusions are visible, because moving a

11
00:00:36.073 --> 00:00:40.201
stage outside the analysis can move its
impacts out of sight.

12
00:00:40.322 --> 00:00:45.515
The inventory records energy and material
inputs plus environmental releases.

13
00:00:45.515 --> 00:00:49.946
Impact assessment connects those flows to
potential consequences, and interpretation

14
00:00:49.946 --> 00:00:54.820
asks what drives the result and how
uncertainty affects the decision.

15
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.547
Life-cycle assessment rarely produces a
universal winner.

16
00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:04.601
Its value is revealing trade-offs across the
full system instead of optimizing one stage in

17
00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:05.005
isolation.
